  <section id="sales-system-diagnostic">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Start with the truth</div>
      <h2>Diagnose the system before prescribing the fix.</h2>
      <p>A weak pipeline can have several causes at once. The market may be too broad. The offer may be hard to explain. Good leads may be sitting untouched. Sellers may be advancing deals without evidence. The CRM may hide the gaps. Leadership may be reviewing totals without reviewing decisions.</p>
      <p>The first phase maps the current state across direction, process, people, data and management. It identifies which failures are creating the most commercial drag and which changes must happen first.</p>
      <div class="revenue-diagnostic-grid">
        <article><span>Direction</span><strong>Are we pursuing the right market with a clear offer?</strong></article>
        <article><span>Process</span><strong>Can everyone explain how a lead becomes a real opportunity?</strong></article>
        <article><span>Ownership</span><strong>Does every handoff and next step have one accountable owner?</strong></article>
        <article><span>Data</span><strong>Does the CRM show evidence or repeat seller opinions?</strong></article>
        <article><span>Management</span><strong>Do weekly reviews create decisions and commitments?</strong></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="revenue-layer-section" id="sales-process-design">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">What gets rebuilt</div>
      <h2>Six operating layers. One sales system.</h2>
      <div class="revenue-layer-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>ICP and offer clarity</h3><p>Define the companies, buyers, problems and commercial situations that deserve attention. Make exclusions as clear as targets so the team stops treating every account as equal.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Pipeline stages and qualification</h3><p>Replace vague labels with observable buyer progress. Define the evidence required to enter, advance, hold or close every stage.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Lead routing and handoffs</h3><p>Set ownership, response windows, information requirements and transfer rules so work does not disappear between marketing, SDRs, AEs, founders and managers.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>CRM workflow and pipeline truth</h3><p>Decide what must be recorded, where it belongs and how clean data supports decisions. The CRM becomes the system of record instead of a storage bin.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Reporting and forecasting</h3><p>Measure movement, conversion, aging, coverage, next-step quality and risk. Make the assumptions behind the forecast visible enough to challenge.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Weekly sales management</h3><p>Install a repeatable rhythm for reviewing evidence, fixing bottlenecks, setting priorities and holding owners to specific commitments.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

