  <section class="revenue-faq-section" id="faq">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">B2B sales system Q&amp;A</div>
      <h2>Questions buyers ask about sales systems, RevOps, CRM and the 90-day build.</h2>
      <p class="revenue-section-lead">These are direct answers to the practical questions founders and sales leaders ask before rebuilding the system.</p>

      <div class="revenue-faq-group">
        <h3>Definition and fit</h3>
        <div class="revenue-faq-list">
          <details><summary>What is a B2B sales system?</summary><p>A B2B sales system is the connected set of decisions, rules, tools, roles, and management habits a company uses to turn a target market into qualified pipeline and closed business. It includes the ICP, sales process, pipeline stages, qualification, ownership, CRM workflow, follow-up rules, reporting, and weekly review rhythm.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is the 90-Day Revenue Engine?</summary><p>The 90-Day Revenue Engine is CallTeam's productized B2B sales system consulting engagement. It diagnoses where the sales operation is breaking, rebuilds the process, installs the operating rules, and leaves the company with a practical system it can run after the engagement.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What problems does a sales system consultant fix?</summary><p>A sales system consultant can fix unclear targeting, inconsistent qualification, meaningless pipeline stages, poor lead ownership, weak handoffs, unreliable CRM data, follow-up gaps, weak reporting, inaccurate forecasting, and a lack of management rhythm. The exact work depends on where the current system is failing.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Who is the 90-Day Revenue Engine for?</summary><p>It is built for B2B founders, CEOs, owners, sales leaders, and teams with a real offer but an inconsistent or poorly controlled sales operation. It is especially useful when activity is happening but leadership cannot clearly see what is moving, what is stuck, who owns the next step, or why pipeline is weak.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How do we know if the problem is leads, people, or the sales system?</summary><p>If the team has no viable target accounts, the lead problem comes first. If the process is clear but sellers cannot execute calls, discovery, objections, or follow-up, the people problem comes first. If targeting, stages, ownership, CRM records, follow-up, reporting, and management are all inconsistent, the sales system needs to be rebuilt.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can the Revenue Engine help us move beyond founder-led sales?</summary><p>Yes. The engagement can help turn the founder's unwritten knowledge into a defined company process, including target-market decisions, qualification standards, pipeline stages, handoffs, follow-up rules, playbooks, reporting, and weekly inspection. The founder may remain involved, but the system becomes easier for other people to understand and run.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is the Revenue Engine only for startups or SaaS companies?</summary><p>No. It can support B2B technology, professional services, manufacturing, logistics, cybersecurity, IT services, agencies, and other companies with a defined offer and a sales process that needs more structure. Fit depends more on the sales problem than the industry label.</p></details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="revenue-faq-group">
        <h3>Process and deliverables</h3>
        <div class="revenue-faq-list">
          <details><summary>Why does the engagement last 90 days?</summary><p>Ninety days creates enough time to inspect the current operation, make the important decisions, install the new workflow, run it with real opportunities, correct what does not work, and transfer a documented system. It is a focused quarter, not an open-ended consulting arrangement.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this the same as a 30-60-90-day sales plan?</summary><p>No. A 30-60-90-day sales plan usually describes how one new seller will learn and perform in a role. The 90-Day Revenue Engine is a company-level sales system buildout covering process, pipeline, ownership, CRM workflow, reporting, playbooks, and management rhythm.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What happens in the first 30 days?</summary><p>The first 30 days focus on diagnosis and design. CallTeam reviews the offer, ICP, process, pipeline, CRM, roles, handoffs, reporting, and current operating habits, then builds a prioritized leak map and a practical sales operating blueprint.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What does CallTeam actually build?</summary><p>Core deliverables can include a revenue-system diagnostic, pipeline leak map, ICP decisions, sales-process architecture, stage definitions, qualification criteria, ownership and handoff rules, follow-up standards, reporting scorecard, weekly review cadence, operating playbook, and next-quarter plan.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this strategy only, or does CallTeam help implement the changes?</summary><p>It is designed as a hands-on buildout, not a strategy deck. CallTeam helps define, document, install, test, and refine the agreed system. The exact implementation scope, including CRM configuration or dashboard work, is set before the engagement begins.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can CallTeam work with our existing CRM?</summary><p>Yes. The Revenue Engine can work around an existing CRM and help clarify fields, stages, required evidence, ownership, next-step rules, reporting, and inspection habits. A complete migration or complex enterprise implementation is a separate scope decision.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can the Revenue Engine fix our pipeline stages?</summary><p>Yes. Pipeline stages can be redesigned around observable buyer progress instead of seller hope. Each stage should have a clear meaning, entry rule, exit rule, owner, required evidence, and next step so leadership can trust what the pipeline shows.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can it improve lead routing, follow-up, and sales handoffs?</summary><p>Yes. The work can define where a lead goes, who owns it, how quickly action is required, what follow-up must happen, what information moves with it, when ownership changes, and how missed or stalled opportunities return to the right queue.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can the Revenue Engine improve reporting and forecasting?</summary><p>Yes. It can install a smaller set of useful measures, clarify data requirements, improve stage confidence, and create a consistent review process. It cannot make an uncertain forecast certain, but it can make the assumptions, evidence, risks, and gaps far more visible.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is a weekly revenue operating rhythm?</summary><p>A weekly revenue operating rhythm is a repeatable management cadence for reviewing pipeline movement, next steps, stage evidence, follow-up gaps, conversion patterns, forecast risk, and owner commitments. It turns CRM data into decisions and accountability instead of another dashboard nobody uses.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What will our team own after day 90?</summary><p>The company should own a documented and working sales operating system: clear target-market decisions, pipeline definitions, qualification rules, ownership, CRM standards, scorecards, review routines, playbooks, and a prioritized plan for the next quarter.</p></details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="revenue-faq-group">
        <h3>Comparisons, buying and results</h3>
        <div class="revenue-faq-list">
          <details><summary>How is sales system consulting different from Revenue Operations consulting?</summary><p>Sales system consulting focuses on how the sales operation works. Revenue Operations can cover a wider company system across marketing, sales, customer success, finance, technology, and data. The Revenue Engine stays centered on the B2B sales process while connecting with other functions where handoffs affect it.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How is the Revenue Engine different from hiring a CRM consultant?</summary><p>A CRM consultant may configure software, fields, automations, and dashboards. The Revenue Engine first defines the sales decisions and operating process the CRM should support. Software should reflect the sales system, not become a substitute for one.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How is the Revenue Engine different from fractional sales leadership?</summary><p>Fractional sales leadership often provides ongoing executive management, coaching, hiring support, forecasting, and team leadership. The Revenue Engine is a fixed 90-day system buildout. It can include leadership discipline, but its main outcome is a company-owned operating model rather than an indefinite executive role.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this better than hiring another SDR or sales manager?</summary><p>It depends on the problem. If the system is sound and the team simply needs capacity, hiring may be right. If the ICP is unclear, stages are unreliable, ownership is loose, CRM data is weak, and follow-up is inconsistent, adding people can add cost without fixing the underlying operation.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How is the Revenue Engine different from CallTeam's other services?</summary><p>Outbound Calling executes calling and follow-up. AI Lead Generation improves targeting, research, and prioritization. Sales Execution Lab improves how people sell. The 90-Day Revenue Engine rebuilds the wider sales system that connects ICP, process, pipeline, CRM, ownership, reporting, playbooks, and management.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How is progress measured?</summary><p>Progress is measured through agreed implementation and operating indicators. These can include stage adoption, required-data completion, follow-up compliance, clear next-step coverage, pipeline aging, stage conversion, handoff quality, forecast confidence, and completion of the operating playbook. Revenue remains a business outcome influenced by factors beyond one consulting engagement.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Does CallTeam guarantee pipeline or revenue?</summary><p>No honest sales consultant can control buyer demand, product-market fit, pricing, competition, economic conditions, client adoption, or every seller decision. CallTeam commits to the agreed diagnostic, buildout, implementation support, operating discipline, and measurement defined in the engagement.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How much time does the client team need to provide?</summary><p>The client needs to provide access to the right leaders, systems, process information, and operating evidence, then participate in key decisions and adoption. The exact time depends on company size and scope, but the engagement requires an internal owner who can make decisions and keep the team moving.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How is pricing determined?</summary><p>Pricing depends on the size of the sales operation, number of roles and markets, current system complexity, CRM and reporting scope, implementation depth, leadership support required, and the condition of the existing process. The diagnostic call is used to define the right scope before a proposal is made.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What happens after the 90-day engagement?</summary><p>The company receives the agreed operating system, documentation, scorecards, management cadence, and next-quarter priorities. Some clients continue with a separate support arrangement or another CallTeam service, while others operate the system internally.</p></details>
          <details><summary>When is the Revenue Engine not the right fit?</summary><p>It may not be the right fit when the offer has not been validated, leadership will not participate, nobody can own implementation, the company only wants a CRM technician, or the immediate problem is simply a lack of calling capacity or a narrow seller skill gap.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How do we start?</summary><p>Start with a Revenue System Diagnostic call. CallTeam will review the company, offer, buyers, sales team, pipeline, CRM, current process, visible breakdowns, and next-quarter priorities to determine whether a 90-day buildout is the right move.</p></details>
        </div>
